    {
      "source": "hackernews",
      "subsource": "news.ycombinator.com",
      "category": "ai",
      "title": "OpenAI, the Partition Principle, and Mathematics",
      "url": "https://karagila.org/2026/openai-pp/",
      "discuss_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50013902",
      "score": 141,
      "comments": 220,
      "author": "md224",
      "published": "2026-10-08T23:29:43Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Explores using frontier LLM reasoning systems to tackle open foundational set theory problems, specifically whether the Partition Principle implies Choice.",
        "Assesses AI capabilities in formal mathematics, contrasting syntactic proof generation in systems like Lean against deep semantic mathematical breakthroughs.",
        "Highlights current model limitations: LLMs excel at search and local lemma verification but struggle with novel global proof strategies in ZFC."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/programming",
      "category": "engineering",
      "title": "Anti-Patterns in Software Blogging",
      "url": "https://refactoringenglish.com/blog/anti-patterns-software-blogging/",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/rwdufq",
      "score": 142,
      "comments": 52,
      "author": "mtlynch",
      "published": "2026-10-07T13:09:33Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Surface-level 'Hello World' tutorials and unverified AI-generated code snippets degrade credibility and ignore real production trade-offs.",
        "Omitting benchmarks, failure modes, and architectural edge cases limits utility for engineers evaluating frameworks or system designs.",
        "Paywalls, disabled RSS feeds, bloated JavaScript frameworks, and aggressive popups alienate developers seeking reproducible, lightweight technical writeups."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/security",
      "category": "security",
      "title": "64-Day Certificate Lifetimes Coming Feb 2027",
      "url": "https://letsencrypt.org/2026/10/07/64-day-certs.html",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/dcrosf",
      "score": 26,
      "comments": 9,
      "author": "fanf",
      "published": "2026-10-08T19:06:25Z",
      "bullets": [
        "TLS certificate validity drops from 398 to 64 days in Feb 2027, driven by browser root program policy updates.",
        "Manual rotations become unviable; organizations must adopt automated issuance protocols like ACME via Certbot or HashiCorp Vault.",
        "Dramatically reduces exposure windows for compromised private keys and minimizes reliance on ineffective OCSP and CRL revocation checks."
      ]
    },
